const e=[{id:"sv-5-926",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does an Xbar chart detect a shift in the process mean faster than an Individuals chart of the same process?",options:["The Xbar chart applies more tests for special causes than the Individuals chart","The Xbar chart uses specification limits, so any shift is compared with the tolerance","Subgroup averages vary more than single values, so the Xbar limits are wider","Subgroup averages vary less than single values, so the Xbar limits are tighter"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the spread of averages with the spread of individual values.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An average of several units varies **less** than single units, so the Xbar chart has **tighter limits**. The book says this gives **greater sensitivity** than an I-MR chart."},{title:"Apply",body:"With tighter limits, a given shift in the mean moves the plotted averages outside the limits sooner."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Averages do not vary more; specifications are never used for control limits; both charts use the same family of tests."}],final:"Averages vary less → tighter limits → faster detection → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the same tests for special causes are available on both charts; sensitivity comes from the tighter limits.","Incorrect — control limits come from process data; specification limits are never drawn on an Xbar chart.","Incorrect — this reverses the effect; averaging reduces spread, which narrows the limits.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 524, 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-927",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about Xbar-R control limits and specification limits is CORRECT?",options:["A process can be in statistical control and still produce units outside the specification","Control limits are set by the customer and specification limits by the process data","A process in statistical control always produces units inside the specification","A subgroup average outside the specification limits is the definition of Test 1"],answer:0,hint:"Control limits are the voice of the process; specifications are the voice of the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits show what the process **does** (stability). Specifications show what the customer **wants** (capability). The two are independent."},{title:"Apply",body:"A stable process with too much spread or an off-target mean stays inside its control limits while still making out-of-spec units — stable is not the same as capable."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The roles of customer and process are reversed in one option; Test 1 refers to the 3-sigma control limits, not to specifications."}],final:"Stable ≠ capable → in control yet out of spec is possible → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this swaps them: control limits come from process data, specifications from the customer or business.","Incorrect — stability says nothing about meeting specifications; that is a capability question.","Incorrect — Test 1 is a point beyond the 3-sigma control limits, not beyond the specification limits."],trap:"Stable (in control) and capable (inside specification) are different questions.",bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 524, 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-928",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A packaging line prints this Xbar-R output for label position. Subgroup 18 averaged 13.4 mm, inside the specification.

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Label Position (mm)    n = 5, 25 subgroups
Specification (individual labels): 9.0 to 15.0 mm

R Chart:     UCL = 3.70    CL = 1.75    LCL = 0
Test Results for R Chart: No test failures.

Xbar Chart:  UCL = 13.01   CL = 12.00   LCL = 10.99
Test Results for Xbar Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 18     (Xbar = 13.4)
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The control limits should be widened to the specification limits to avoid false alarms","The R chart must be recalculated, because the Xbar chart has failed a test","No action is needed, because every subgroup average is inside the specification limits","Subgroup 18 shows a special cause that should be investigated, even though it is within specification"],answer:3,hint:"Read the R chart first, then judge Xbar points against the control limits, not the specification.",steps:[{title:"Read the R chart",body:"No test failures, so the Xbar limits (built from R̄) can be trusted."},{title:"Read the Xbar chart",body:"Subgroup 18 (13.4 mm) is above the UCL of 13.01 mm — a Test 1 special-cause signal."},{title:"Decide",body:"Being inside the specification does not cancel a control-chart signal; the cause should be found."}],final:"Test 1 at subgroup 18 → investigate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — specification limits never replace control limits; doing so would hide genuine special causes.","Incorrect — the R chart shows no failures; a signal on the Xbar chart is no reason to recalculate the R chart.","Incorrect — this judges the chart against specifications; a point beyond the control limits is a signal whatever the specification.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 524, 530–531; Book p. 532"},{id:"sv-5-929",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"After 25 subgroups an Xbar-R chart of fastener torque (n = 4) has a stable R chart. A new subgroup plots above the Xbar UCL. The Green Belt deleted the point and recalculated the limits without looking into it. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because a single point outside the limits is usually a false alarm","Yes, because control limits should be updated whenever new data are added","No, because the point signals a special cause that should be investigated first","No, because the R chart must be recalculated before the Xbar chart"],answer:2,hint:"What does a point beyond the UCL mean, and when may limits be recalculated?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A point beyond 3-sigma limits is unlikely by chance: it is a **special-cause signal** to investigate (Test 1)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Deleting the point and recalculating hides the signal. During monitoring the limits stay fixed and are recalculated only after a documented process change."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The R chart is already stable, so there is nothing to recalculate there."}],final:"Investigate the signal; do not recalculate → No → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 3-sigma limits make a false alarm rare; the point is treated as a special cause until shown otherwise.","Incorrect — monitoring limits are frozen; they are recalculated only after a documented, lasting change.","","Incorrect — the R chart is stable; the action needed concerns the Xbar signal, not the R chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 524, 530–531; Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-930",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"An Xbar chart can still be used when individual values are moderately skewed, because subgroup averages tend toward a normal distribution.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Think of the central limit theorem: what happens to the distribution of averages?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The central limit theorem says that averages tend toward a normal distribution whatever the shape of the individual values."},{title:"Apply",body:"The Xbar chart plots subgroup averages, so moderate skew in the individual data has much less effect on it. The statement is true."}],final:"Averages tend to normal (CLT), so the statement is True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the Xbar chart plots averages, and the central limit theorem makes averages close to normal even for moderately skewed data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270, 530"},{id:"sv-5-931",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does a u chart plot for each subgroup?",options:["The total number of defects in a sample of fixed size","The average number of defects per unit inspected","The proportion of units that are defective","The number of defective units in a sample of fixed size"],answer:1,hint:"u stands for a rate of defects, not a count of bad units.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A u chart plots **defects per unit**: the defects counted in a subgroup divided by the number of units inspected."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A proportion defective is a p chart, a total defect count with fixed size is a c chart, and a count of defective units is an np chart."}],final:"u = defects per unit → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a total defect count in a fixed-size sample is plotted on a c chart.","","Incorrect — the proportion defective is plotted on a p chart.","Incorrect — a count of defective units in a fixed sample is plotted on an np chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-932",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which distribution is the basis for the control limits of a u chart?",options:["Binomial","Exponential","Normal","Poisson"],answer:3,hint:"Which distribution models counts of events, such as defects, where the variance equals the mean?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defect counts follow the **Poisson** distribution, where the variance equals the mean. That is why the u-chart limits use the square root of ū divided by n."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Binomial underlies p and np charts (defective or not); exponential models time between events; normal underlies charts for continuous data."}],final:"Defect counts → Poisson → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the binomial distribution underlies p and np charts, where each unit is defective or not.","Incorrect — the exponential distribution models the time between events, not the number of defects.","Incorrect — the normal distribution underlies charts for continuous measurements such as Xbar-R.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-933",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's example, a hospital claim form has 13 fields. How are the unit and a defect defined for the u chart?",options:["Each form is a unit, and each blank or wrong field on it is a defect","Each day is a unit, and each form with an error is a defect","Each form is a unit, and each form with any error is a defect","Each field is a unit, and each rejected form is a defect"],answer:0,hint:"One unit can hold several defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"On a u chart the **unit** is the item searched (here a claim form) and a **defect** is each individual flaw found on it."},{title:"Apply",body:"A form with three blank fields contributes three defects, and the hospital plots defects per form for each day."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Counting a form once if it has any error turns the data into defectives, which belong on a p chart."}],final:"Unit = form, defect = each bad field → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the day is the subgroup, not the unit; the units are the forms checked that day.","Incorrect — counting each form once describes a defective, which is p-chart data, not u-chart data.","Incorrect — this reverses the roles; the form is the unit searched and each faulty field is a defect."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-934",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On days when more units are inspected, the u-chart limits sit closer to the centre line. Why?",options:["The specification is tighter on days with higher production","A larger sample gives a more precise estimate of the defect rate","Minitab rescales each day so that all points fit on the chart","A larger sample contains more defects, which raises the centre line"],answer:1,hint:"The sample size appears under the square root in the u-chart limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each subgroup's limits use its own n. A bigger sample estimates the defect rate more precisely, so the limits are **narrower**."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why u-chart limits look like steps: tight on big days, wide on small days."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The centre line ū is fixed from the baseline; specifications and rescaling have nothing to do with control limits."}],final:"Larger n → more precise rate → narrower limits → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — control limits come from process data; specifications never set them.","","Incorrect — the limits are calculated from ū and each n, not rescaled for display.","Incorrect — the centre line ū is fixed from the baseline totals; it does not move with each day's sample."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-935",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why does the book say that a u chart 'can do everything a c chart can'?",options:["It works whether the number of units inspected is constant or varies","It needs no centre line, so it can be set up with fewer data","It counts both defects and defective units on the same chart","It applies all eight tests for special causes, unlike the c chart"],answer:0,hint:"What does the c chart require that the u chart does not?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A c chart counts defects but needs a **constant** amount inspected. A u chart divides by the units inspected, so it works with **constant or variable** sample sizes."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Neither chart counts defectives; both have a centre line; both use the same basic tests."}],final:"u handles constant and variable n → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the u chart has a centre line, ū, calculated from total defects over total units.","Incorrect — u and c charts both count defects; defective units belong on p and np charts.","Incorrect — attribute charts such as u and c use the same four basic tests."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-936",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which describes the 'unit' on a u chart?",options:["The smallest defect that the inspector is able to detect","The customer's specification for the maximum defects per item","The number of subgroups used to calculate the control limits","The defined amount of product or service that is searched for defects, such as one form or one page"],answer:3,hint:"The book's examples include a page of typing, a truck door and a claim form.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **unit** (search area) is whatever the team defines as one item to search: a page, a door, a form."},{title:"Apply",body:"Defects found are divided by the number of these units to give u."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Detection size, number of subgroups and specifications are different ideas."}],final:"Unit = defined search area → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that describes the resolution of the inspection, not the unit being searched.","Incorrect — a specification is a customer requirement and is never part of a control chart.","Incorrect — the number of subgroups is used for the baseline, but it is not the unit inspected.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-937",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"On a u chart, Monday and Tuesday both have u = 0.30 defects per unit. Monday plots inside its limits, but Tuesday plots above its UCL. What is the MOST likely reason?",options:["More units were inspected on Tuesday, so Tuesday's limits were narrower","Fewer defects were found on Tuesday than on Monday","Fewer units were inspected on Tuesday, so Tuesday's limits were narrower","Tuesday had a higher centre line than Monday"],answer:0,hint:"Each point is judged against its own limits, which depend on that day's n.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"u-chart limits vary with each subgroup's n: **more units → narrower limits**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The same rate can sit inside wide limits (small n) and outside narrow limits (large n). Tuesday must have had the larger sample."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Fewer units would widen the limits; the centre line is the same every day; fewer defects at the same u still means the same rate."}],final:"Same u, larger n → tighter UCL → signal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the plotted rate is the same on both days; the difference lies in the limits, not the count.","Incorrect — fewer units would make Tuesday's limits wider, not narrower.","Incorrect — ū is a single centre line for all subgroups."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-938",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which tests for special causes does Minitab apply to a u chart?",options:["Tests 1 to 8","Tests 1 to 4 only","Test 1 only","Tests 5 to 8 only"],answer:1,hint:"Attribute charts use the basic tests; the zone tests are for charts of continuous data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For attribute charts (p, np, c, u) Minitab offers **tests 1–4**: a point beyond 3σ, 9 in a row on one side, 6 in a row trending, 14 alternating."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Tests 5–8 (zone tests) are used on Xbar and I charts; Test 1 alone ignores the run tests."}],final:"Attribute charts → tests 1–4 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — tests 5–8 are not used on attribute charts.","","Incorrect — Test 1 is only one of the four tests used; run and trend tests also apply.","Incorrect — tests 5–8 are the zone tests that attribute charts do not use."],bookRef:"Book p. 532; Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-939",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A single fibre installation is audited and three separate wiring faults are found. How many defects and defectives does this represent?",options:["One defect and three defectives","One defect and one defective","Three defects and one defective","Three defects and three defectives"],answer:2,hint:"A defect is a flaw; a defective is a whole unit that fails.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each fault is a **defect**. The installation as a whole is one **defective** unit, however many faults it has."},{title:"Apply",body:"Three faults on one installation → 3 defects, 1 defective. Defects go on c or u charts, defectives on p or np charts."}],final:"3 defects, 1 defective → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this swaps the terms; the faults are defects and the installation is the defective unit.","Incorrect — each fault is a separate defect, so there are three defects, not one.","","Incorrect — a unit is counted as defective only once, however many defects it has."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-940",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the lower control limit of a u chart is CORRECT?",options:["The LCL is fixed at zero for every u chart, whatever the sample size","The LCL is always as far below the centre line as the UCL is above it, even when negative","A point below the LCL can be ignored, because fewer defects are always good news","When the calculated LCL is negative it is set to zero, because a defect rate cannot be below zero"],answer:3,hint:"Think about small samples, where ū minus three standard errors can drop below zero.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For small samples the formula can give a negative LCL. A rate cannot be negative, so the LCL is **set to 0**."},{title:"Apply",body:"With larger samples the LCL can be positive, and a point below it is a signal to investigate (real improvement or an inspection problem)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Negative limits are never kept; low points are not ignored; the LCL is not always zero."}],final:"Negative LCL → set to 0 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — with large enough samples the u-chart LCL is above zero.","Incorrect — a negative limit is meaningless for a rate, so it is replaced by zero.","Incorrect — a point below the LCL is a signal; it may be a real improvement or missed defects, so it is investigated.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-941",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about u charts and p charts is CORRECT?",options:["Both charts require the same sample size in every subgroup","A p chart counts every defect found on a unit separately","A u chart monitors defects, while a p chart monitors defective units","A u chart is used when each unit is simply classed as pass or fail"],answer:2,hint:"Defects (u, c) versus defectives (p, np).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**u** = defects per unit (several per unit possible). **p** = proportion of units that are defective (pass/fail)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Both charts allow a varying sample size; pass/fail data and counting each unit once belong to the p chart."}],final:"u = defects, p = defectives → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both u and p charts handle varying sample sizes; c and np need a constant size.","Incorrect — a p chart counts each unit once as defective or not; counting every defect is u or c data.","","Incorrect — pass/fail data per unit are defectives, which belong on a p or np chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541; Book p. 521"},{id:"sv-5-942",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about interpreting a u chart is CORRECT?",options:["Points below the centre line show that the process meets the customer's requirements","A point well below the LCL may be a real improvement or an inspection problem, so it should be investigated","A point that falls inside its limits on a high-volume day should be judged against the widest limit on the chart","Points should be compared with the customer's maximum defect rate rather than with the control limits"],answer:1,hint:"Signals can occur in either direction, and each point has its own limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A point beyond either limit signals a special cause. Below the LCL it may mean a real improvement — or inspectors missing defects. Both are investigated."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Being below the centre line says nothing about requirements; each point is judged against its **own** limits; customer limits do not belong on control charts."}],final:"Low signal → investigate both explanations → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the centre line is the process average, not a customer requirement; stability is not capability.","","Incorrect — each u-chart point is judged against the limits calculated for its own sample size.","Incorrect — control charts compare points with control limits; customer limits belong in a capability study."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-943",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the u chart are CORRECT?

1. Each unit inspected can contain more than one defect.
2. The plotted value is the number of defects divided by the number of units inspected.
3. The number of units inspected must be the same in every subgroup.
4. It is the right chart for the proportion of late deliveries each day.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Defects per unit, with a sample size that may vary.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 true: a unit can have several defects. 2 true: u = defects ÷ units. 3 false: the u chart allows a varying sample size (that is its advantage over c). 4 false: late or not late is a defective, so a p chart is used."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"Only 1 and 2 are both true."}],final:"1 and 2 true → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes statement 3, but a u chart does not need a constant sample size.","Incorrect — both are false: n may vary on a u chart, and late deliveries are defectives for a p chart.","Incorrect — includes statement 4; a delivery is either late or not, which is p-chart data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-944",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about the control limits of a u chart are CORRECT?

1. They are calculated from the customer's specification.
2. They change from subgroup to subgroup when the number of units inspected changes.
3. They are narrower for subgroups in which more units were inspected.
4. They are recalculated each time a point falls outside them.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Limits come from ū and each subgroup's n.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false: limits come from process data, never from specifications. 2 true: each subgroup's n is used. 3 true: larger n gives narrower limits. 4 false: a signal is investigated; limits are recalculated only after a documented change."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"2 and 3 are both true."}],final:"2 and 3 true → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 1; specifications are never used to calculate control limits.","","Incorrect — includes statement 4; a point outside the limits is investigated, not used to reset the limits.","Incorrect — both are false: specifications do not set limits, and signals do not trigger recalculation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541; Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-945",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A u chart is used to monitor the number of [?] per unit when the number of units inspected varies.`,options:["defectives","defects","proportions","subgroups"],answer:1,hint:"One unit can carry several of these.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The u chart tracks **defects** per unit, where a unit can contain more than one defect."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Defectives are whole failed units (p and np charts); proportions and subgroups do not fit the sentence."}],final:"u chart = defects per unit → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — defectives are whole units that fail; they are tracked on p and np charts.","","Incorrect — a proportion is what a p chart plots, not something counted per unit.","Incorrect — a subgroup is the sample itself, not what is counted on each unit."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-946",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Because u-chart limits depend on each subgroup's sample size, the plotted limits look [?] when the sample sizes differ.`,options:["curved","dashed","stepped","symmetrical"],answer:2,hint:"Picture limits that change at every subgroup.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each subgroup has its own n, so its UCL and LCL change from point to point. The limit lines therefore look **stepped**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The limits jump between subgroups rather than curving; line style is not the point; once the LCL is set to zero the limits are no longer symmetrical."}],final:"Variable n → stepped limits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the limits change in steps at each subgroup, not along a smooth curve.","Incorrect — line style is a display choice, not a property caused by changing sample sizes.","","Incorrect — limits are not always symmetrical; a negative LCL is set to zero, which breaks the symmetry."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-947",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which situation is NOT suited to a u chart?",options:["Typing errors per page, with a different number of pages checked each day","Wiring faults per installation, with a different number of installations audited each week","Paint blemishes per car door, with a different number of doors inspected each shift","The proportion of invoices with at least one error, with a different number of invoices each day"],answer:3,hint:"Three options count flaws per unit; one classes each unit only as good or bad.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A u chart needs **defects per unit** (several possible per unit) with a sample size that may vary."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"'Invoices with at least one error' classes each invoice as defective or not, so the data are a **proportion defective** → p chart."}],final:"Proportion of invoices with an error → p chart, not u → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — typing errors per page with a varying number of pages is one of the book's u-chart examples.","Incorrect — several wiring faults can occur on one installation, and the number audited varies: u chart.","Incorrect — blemishes per door with varying doors is a classic u-chart case.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-948",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which information is NOT needed to draw a u chart?",options:["The customer's specification for defects per unit","The number of defects found in each subgroup","The number of units inspected in each subgroup","The time order in which the subgroups were collected"],answer:0,hint:"A control chart is built from process data only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A u chart needs, for each subgroup, the **defects counted** and the **units inspected**, plotted in **time order**."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"A customer specification is never used to set or draw control limits."}],final:"Specification not needed → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the defect count is the numerator of each plotted u value.","Incorrect — the units inspected are the denominator of u and also set each subgroup's limits.","Incorrect — a control chart plots subgroups in time order so that runs and trends can be seen."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-949",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"A u chart of defects per circuit board signals on day 14. Which action should the team NOT take?",options:["Record what was different about materials, people or machines on day 14","Follow the out-of-control action plan written for the chart","Recalculate the limits straight away so that day 14 falls inside them","Check whether the inspection method or inspector changed on day 14"],answer:2,hint:"A signal is investigated, not hidden.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A signal means a likely special cause. The team investigates (including the measurement), records findings and follows the OCAP."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"Recalculating the limits to absorb the point hides the signal; limits are recalculated only after a documented process change."}],final:"Do not recalculate to hide the signal → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — recording what differed on day 14 is how the special cause is found.","Incorrect — the OCAP exists precisely to guide the response to a signal.","","Incorrect — a change in inspection is a common cause of attribute-chart signals and should be checked."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541; Book pp. 547–548, 553"},{id:"sv-5-950",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A paint shop inspects a different number of car doors each shift and records every blemish found. A door can have several blemishes. Which control chart should be used?",options:["U chart","C chart","P chart","NP chart"],answer:0,hint:"Defects or defectives? Constant or varying sample?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Blemishes are **defects** (several per door) and the number of doors **varies** → **u chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A c chart needs a constant number inspected; p and np track defective units, not defects."}],final:"Defects + varying n → U chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a c chart needs the same number of doors every shift.","Incorrect — a p chart tracks the proportion of defective doors, ignoring how many blemishes each has.","Incorrect — an np chart counts defective doors in a fixed sample."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-951",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A software team counts the defects found in each week's code reviews. The amount of code reviewed varies from 4 to 15 thousand lines a week, and the team wants a chart of defects per thousand lines. Which chart fits?",options:["P chart","I-MR chart of the weekly defect totals","C chart","U chart"],answer:3,hint:"Here the 'unit' is one thousand lines of code.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defects are counted, and the amount inspected (thousands of lines) **varies** each week. Dividing by that amount gives defects per unit → **u chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A c chart needs a constant amount reviewed; a p chart needs pass/fail units; plotting raw weekly totals ignores the changing amount of code."}],final:"Defects per varying amount → U chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a p chart needs each unit classed as defective or not, not defects counted.","Incorrect — raw weekly totals rise and fall with the amount reviewed, so they would give false signals.","Incorrect — a c chart assumes the same amount of code is reviewed every week.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-952",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A call-centre quality team checks each sampled call against 10 script items and records how many items were missed. The number of calls checked varies each day. The manager wants to track missed items per call. Which chart should be used?",options:["P chart","U chart","C chart","NP chart"],answer:1,hint:"Is the manager tracking bad calls or missed items?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each missed item is a **defect**, and one call can have several. The rate wanted is **missed items per call**, with a **varying** number of calls → **u chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A p chart would track the proportion of calls with any miss, losing the count of items; np needs constant n and defectives; c needs a constant number of calls."}],final:"Missed items per call, varying calls → U chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a p chart would show the share of calls with at least one miss, not missed items per call.","","Incorrect — a c chart needs the same number of calls checked every day.","Incorrect — an np chart counts defective calls in a constant sample."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-953",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A hospital tracks medication errors each month. Several errors can involve the same patient, and the number of patient-days varies from month to month. The team wants a chart of errors per 100 patient-days. Which chart should it use?",options:["U chart","NP chart","C chart","P chart"],answer:0,hint:"Define the unit as 100 patient-days.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Errors are **defects**, the area of opportunity (patient-days) **varies**, and the rate wanted is errors per unit → **u chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"np and p track defective units; a c chart would need the same number of patient-days every month."}],final:"Errors per varying patient-days → U chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an np chart counts defective units in a fixed sample, not errors per patient-day.","Incorrect — a c chart needs a constant area of opportunity, but patient-days vary.","Incorrect — a p chart tracks the proportion of defective units, not a rate of errors."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-954",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"Housekeeping audits inspect between 8 and 25 hotel rooms a day and count every defect found. A supervisor proposes plotting the total defects per day on a c chart. Was this an appropriate proposal?",options:["Yes, because a c chart counts defects, which is what the audit records","Yes, because daily totals are easier for staff to understand than rates","No, because a c chart needs a constant number of rooms, so a u chart is needed","No, because defects must always be charted as proportions on a p chart"],answer:2,hint:"What does a c chart assume about the amount inspected?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A c chart counts defects but assumes the **same amount** is inspected each time. Here the rooms vary from 8 to 25."},{title:"Apply",body:"Daily totals would rise and fall with the number of rooms. Dividing by rooms inspected — a **u chart** — removes that effect."}],final:"Varying rooms → u chart, not c → No → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — counting defects is right, but the c chart also needs a constant number of rooms inspected.","Incorrect — ease of reading does not fix the false signals caused by a changing number of rooms.","","Incorrect — defects are charted on c or u charts; p charts are for defective units."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-955",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A u chart of defects per circuit board shows a point above its UCL on Tuesday. What should the Green Belt do FIRST?",options:["Adjust the process so that Wednesday's defect rate falls below the centre line","Investigate what was different on Tuesday, including materials, people and the inspection method","Switch to a p chart, which is less affected by single points","Add Tuesday's data and recalculate the control limits"],answer:1,hint:"Signal → find the special cause before changing anything.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A point above the UCL is a special-cause signal. The first step is to **investigate** what was different, guided by the OCAP."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculating hides the signal; changing chart type does not explain it; adjusting without a known cause is tampering."}],final:"Investigate the special cause first → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — adjusting without knowing the cause is tampering and adds variation.","","Incorrect — a p chart is for defectives; changing the chart does not explain Tuesday's defects.","Incorrect — limits are not recalculated to absorb a signal; that hides the special cause."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541; Book pp. 547–548"},{id:"sv-5-956",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints part of a u chart of defects per audited cabinet:

\`\`\`
U Chart of Cabinet Defects    U-bar = 0.500
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes

Point   Units   Defects     U       UCL     LCL
  7       10       6      0.600   1.171   0
  8      100      45      0.450   0.712   0.288
\`\`\`

Why is the LCL 0 for point 7 but 0.288 for point 8?`,options:["Point 8 uses a different centre line from point 7","Point 8 is out of control, so Minitab raised its LCL","Point 7 had few units, so its calculated LCL was negative and was set to zero","Point 7 had more defects, so Minitab lowered its LCL to zero"],answer:2,hint:"Each subgroup's limits depend on its own number of units.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Both points share U-bar = 0.500. Point 7 has 10 units, point 8 has 100."},{title:"Interpret",body:"With only 10 units the 3-sigma band is wide, so ū minus that band is below zero and the LCL is set to **0**. With 100 units the band is narrow, leaving a positive LCL."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Defect counts do not set the limits; point 8 (0.450) is inside its limits; the centre line is the same for all points."}],final:"Small n → negative LCL → 0 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both points use the same centre line, U-bar = 0.500.","Incorrect — point 8 (0.450) lies between 0.288 and 0.712, so it is in control.","","Incorrect — the limits depend on ū and the units inspected, not on the defects found that day."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-957",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team charts installation audit defects on a c chart:

\`\`\`
C Chart of Audit Defects    C-bar = 24.0
UCL = 38.70   LCL = 9.30

Test Results for C Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 1, 6, 11, 16     (C = 44, 47, 41, 45)

Note: points 1, 6, 11 and 16 are Mondays. Monday audits also cover
weekend installations, about twice as many units as other days.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Monday installations have a special cause of poor quality that must be removed","The signals show the process is out of control, so the audit should be stopped on Mondays","The c chart is correct, but its limits should be widened for Mondays","The signals mainly reflect the larger number of units on Mondays, so a u chart should be used"],answer:3,hint:"What does a c chart assume about the amount inspected each day?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Every flagged point is a Monday, and Mondays inspect about twice as many units."},{title:"Interpret",body:"A c chart assumes a **constant** amount inspected. More units naturally give more defects, so the 'signals' come from the varying sample size. Charting defects **per unit** on a u chart removes this effect."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Blaming Monday quality ignores the doubled sample; widening limits by hand is improvised; stopping the audit hides the data."}],final:"Varying units → switch to a u chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Monday counts are high because twice as many units are inspected, not necessarily because quality is worse.","Incorrect — the problem is the choice of chart, not the audit; stopping audits would hide information.","Incorrect — adjusting c-chart limits by hand is improvised; the u chart is the chart designed for a varying sample size.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-958",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A u chart of defects per installation prints:

\`\`\`
U Chart of Installation Defects    U-bar = 1.10
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes

Test Results for U Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 6, 7, 8, 9

Note: from point 6 the audit checklist was extended from 10 to 15 check items.
\`\`\`

What is the MOST likely explanation for the signals?`,options:["The chart is invalid, because u charts cannot be used with more than 10 check items","Installation quality suddenly worsened from point 6, so the installers need retraining","The number of installations fell at point 6, which made the limits too narrow","The longer checklist changed what counts as a defect, so the rise may come from the measurement, not the process"],answer:3,hint:"Did the process change, or did the way defects are counted change?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Points 6–9 all fail Test 1, starting exactly when the checklist grew from 10 to 15 items."},{title:"Interpret",body:"More check items means more opportunities to record a defect, so the measured rate rises even if the installations are unchanged. The signal is real, but its cause is most likely the **measurement system**."},{title:"Next step",body:"Confirm this, document the change and, once it is permanent, recalculate the limits on the new definition."}],final:"Changed defect definition → measurement cause → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — there is no limit on check items; the unit simply has more opportunities for defects.","Incorrect — the timing matches the checklist change, so retraining installers is premature.","Incorrect — fewer installations would widen the limits, not narrow them.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541; Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-959",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps from the book's general procedure for a u chart in order:

- **P** — Set the basis for subgrouping
- **Q** — Decide the purpose of the chart
- **R** — Count the nonconformities in each subgroup
- **S** — Interpret the chart together with other process information`,options:["Q → R → P → S","R → Q → P → S","Q → P → R → S","P → Q → R → S"],answer:2,hint:"Purpose first, interpretation last.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's procedure starts by **deciding the purpose**, then sets the collection point, **subgrouping**, sampling and forms, then **counts nonconformities**, enters the data and finally **interprets**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (purpose) → P (subgrouping) → R (count) → S (interpret)."}],final:"Purpose → subgrouping → count → interpret → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — defects cannot be counted per subgroup before the subgroups are defined.","Incorrect — counting comes after the purpose and subgroups are set, not first.","","Incorrect — subgrouping cannot be designed before the purpose of the chart is decided."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 530–531, 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-960",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which data are entered for each subgroup to create a p chart?",options:["The number of defects and the area inspected","The number of defective units and the subgroup size","The individual measurements and the subgroup size","The number of defects per unit and the number of subgroups"],answer:1,hint:"A p chart turns a count of bad units into a proportion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's input for a p chart is, for each subgroup, the **number defective** (an integer) and the **sample size**. Minitab divides one by the other."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Defects per unit and defects per area are u/c-chart data; individual measurements are continuous data for Xbar or I-MR charts."}],final:"Number defective + sample size → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — defects and the area inspected are the inputs for a u chart.","","Incorrect — individual measurements are continuous data for Xbar-R, Xbar-S or I-MR charts.","Incorrect — defects per unit are u-chart data; a p chart needs defectives and each subgroup's size."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-961",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which distribution underlies the control limits of a p chart?",options:["Binomial","Exponential","Normal","Poisson"],answer:0,hint:"Each unit has only two possible outcomes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each unit is **defective or not**, so the number of defectives in a sample follows the **binomial** distribution. The p̄(1 − p̄) term in the limits comes from it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Poisson is for defect counts (c, u); exponential is for time between events; normal is for continuous data."}],final:"Defective or not → binomial → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the exponential distribution models time between events, not the number of defective units.","Incorrect — the normal distribution underlies charts for continuous measurements.","Incorrect — the Poisson distribution underlies c and u charts, which count defects."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-962",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How does the book define a 'defective' for a p chart?",options:["A whole unit that fails acceptance, regardless of how many defects it has","A subgroup whose proportion is above the centre line","A single flaw on a unit, so one unit can be counted several times","A unit whose measurement falls outside the control limits"],answer:0,hint:"Defective describes the unit, not the flaw.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defective** is an entire unit that fails to meet acceptance — counted **once**, however many defects it carries."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single flaw is a defect (u, c charts); control limits and centre lines describe the chart, not the unit."}],final:"Defective = whole unit fails, counted once → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a point above the centre line describes a subgroup's position on the chart, not a defective unit.","Incorrect — a single flaw is a defect; defects are tracked on c and u charts.","Incorrect — control limits apply to plotted statistics; acceptance of a unit is judged against its requirement."],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-5-963",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why does the book say that a p chart 'can do everything an np chart can'?",options:["It can plot continuous measurements as well as counts","It can count several defects on the same unit","It works whether the subgroup size is constant or varies","It needs no control limits when the sample size is constant"],answer:2,hint:"What does the np chart require that the p chart does not?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Both charts track defectives. The np chart needs a **constant** sample size; the p chart plots a proportion, so it handles **constant or varying** sizes."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Counting several defects per unit is u/c data; every control chart has limits; continuous data use variables charts."}],final:"p handles constant and varying n → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — continuous measurements belong on Xbar-R, Xbar-S or I-MR charts.","Incorrect — counting several defects on one unit is defect data for c and u charts.","","Incorrect — a p chart always has control limits; with constant n they are simply straight lines."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-964",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A p chart has daily sample sizes of 40, 120, 300 and 600 units. On which day will the control limits be WIDEST?",options:["The day with 40 units","The day with 120 units","The day with 300 units","The day with 600 units"],answer:0,hint:"The sample size sits in the denominator under the square root.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"p-chart limits are p̄ ± 3 standard errors, and the standard error **shrinks** as n grows. So the **smallest** sample has the **widest** limits."},{title:"Apply",body:"40 units is the smallest sample, so that day has the widest band."}],final:"Smallest n → widest limits → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — 120 units is larger than 40, so its limits are narrower.","Incorrect — 300 units gives a more precise proportion and narrower limits.","Incorrect — the largest sample has the narrowest limits, not the widest."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-965",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When new weeks are added to an existing p chart, why does the book enter the historical p̄ in Minitab's options?",options:["So that Minitab converts the new counts into proportions","So that the limits stay fixed and are not recalculated automatically with the new data","So that Minitab applies all eight tests for special causes","So that the chart shows the customer's target proportion as the centre line"],answer:1,hint:"Monitoring compares new points with the baseline, not with a moving average.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Entering the historical p̄ (Options > Parameters/Estimate) **freezes** the centre line and limits, so new points are judged against the baseline."},{title:"Apply",body:"If Minitab recalculated with every new point, a special cause could drift into the limits and be hidden. Recalculation is a separate, deliberate step."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Targets are never centre lines; conversion to proportions happens anyway; attribute charts use tests 1–4."}],final:"Historical p̄ freezes the limits → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Minitab divides defectives by sample size whether or not the historical p̄ is entered.","","Incorrect — attribute charts use tests 1–4; entering p̄ does not change which tests apply.","Incorrect — the centre line is the historical process average, not a customer target."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547–548"},{id:"sv-5-966",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A 2-proportion test and a p chart both use the proportion defective. How do their purposes differ?",options:["Both compare two groups, but the p chart needs larger samples","The p chart compares two groups, while the 2-proportion test monitors stability over time","Both monitor stability over time, but the test uses specification limits","The p chart monitors stability over time, while the 2-proportion test compares two groups"],answer:3,hint:"Monitoring (Control) versus testing a difference (Analyze).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **p chart** watches one process over time for special causes. A **2-proportion test** asks whether two groups (for example, before and after) have different proportions."},{title:"Apply",body:"The tools answer different questions and are often used in different phases: hypothesis tests in Analyze/Improve, control charts in Control."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The roles are reversed in one option; neither tool uses specification limits; the p chart does not compare two groups."}],final:"p chart = monitoring; 2P test = comparing → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a p chart tracks one process over time; it is not a two-group comparison.","Incorrect — this reverses the roles of the chart and the test.","Incorrect — a hypothesis test compares groups, and neither tool uses specification limits.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548; Book p. 397"},{id:"sv-5-967",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Each invoice can contain several errors, but a p chart counts an invoice only once if it has any error. When is this a drawback?",options:["When the number of errors on each invoice matters, not just whether the invoice is wrong","When the number of invoices checked varies from day to day","When more than 50 invoices are checked each day","When the proportion of wrong invoices is the measure the customer cares about"],answer:0,hint:"A p chart records each unit as defective or not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A p chart sees only **good or bad** units. An invoice with five errors counts the same as one with a single error."},{title:"Apply",body:"If the number of errors matters, the data are **defects per unit** and a u chart fits better."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Varying n and large samples suit a p chart; if the share of wrong invoices is what matters, the p chart is exactly right."}],final:"Error count matters → p chart loses information → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a varying sample size is no problem; the p chart's limits adjust for each n.","Incorrect — the book says p-chart samples are usually larger than 50, so this suits the chart.","Incorrect — if the share of wrong invoices is what matters, the p chart is the right tool."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-968",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which description of the data points to a p chart?",options:["The number of rejected units in a fixed sample of 200","The percentage of orders rejected, with a different number of orders each day","The average fill weight of five bottles every hour","The number of scratches per panel, with the same panel size each time"],answer:1,hint:"Percentage defective plus a varying sample size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Scenario words such as '% rejected' or 'proportion late' with a **changing** number checked point to a **p chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Scratches per panel are defects (c chart); average fill weight is continuous (Xbar-R); a count of rejects in a fixed sample is np."}],final:"% defective + varying n → p chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a count of rejected units in a fixed sample suits an np chart.","","Incorrect — fill weight is continuous data in subgroups of five, which suits an Xbar-R chart.","Incorrect — scratches are defects with a constant area, which suits a c chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-969",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the book's paycheck-rework p chart is CORRECT?",options:["The 25% project goal was not drawn on the chart, because the limits come from the process data","The chart could not be used, because rework is a continuous measure","The control limits were set at the 25% project goal","The UCL was calculated from the customer's maximum rework rate"],answer:0,hint:"The book remarks that specifications were never discussed when building the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The project aimed to cut rework below 25%, but the book notes that **specifications were never discussed**: the p chart's limits come only from process data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Goals and customer limits are never control limits; rework of a paycheck is a pass/fail attribute, not a continuous measure."}],final:"Goal not on the chart; limits from data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — each paycheck either needs rework or not, so the data are attribute (defective) data.","Incorrect — control limits are calculated from p̄ and n, not set at a goal.","Incorrect — customer limits are specifications; they are never used to calculate a UCL."],bookRef:"Book pp. 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-970",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about p-chart control limits is CORRECT?",options:["They are the same for every subgroup, whatever its size","They are calculated from the average number of defects per unit","They are always set three percentage points either side of p̄","They are equally spaced around p̄ unless one of them reaches the natural limit of 0 or 1"],answer:3,hint:"Think about what happens when the lower limit would go below zero.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the limits are **equidistant** from p̄ unless they hit the natural limit of **0 or 1** (0% or 100%). A negative LCL is set to 0."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The width is three standard errors, not three percentage points; the limits change with n; defects per unit belong to the u chart."}],final:"Equidistant unless capped at 0 or 1 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the limits depend on each subgroup's n, so they vary when sample sizes differ.","Incorrect — defects per unit are u-chart data; p-chart limits use the proportion defective.","Incorrect — the width is three standard errors, which depends on p̄ and n, not a fixed three points.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-971",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about when p-chart limits should be recalculated is CORRECT?",options:["Limits are recalculated each time a point falls outside them","Limits are recalculated every week so that they include the latest data","Limits are recalculated only after a documented, lasting change to the process","Limits are recalculated whenever the customer's target changes"],answer:2,hint:"Recalculation is a deliberate step, not a reaction to a signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book makes recalculation a separate, deliberate step: limits are recalculated **after documented process changes**, such as at the end of a project."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculating after a signal hides it; weekly recalculation lets drift creep into the limits; targets have no role in control limits."}],final:"Recalculate only after documented change → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a point outside the limits is investigated through the OCAP, not absorbed by new limits.","Incorrect — rolling recalculation lets a slow drift move the limits and hides the shift.","","Incorrect — customer targets are specifications and never set control limits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547–548, 553"},{id:"sv-5-972",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the p chart are CORRECT?

1. It plots the number of defects per unit.
2. It needs specification limits to calculate the control limits.
3. It can be used when the subgroup size varies.
4. A defective unit is counted once, however many defects it has.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Proportion defective, sample size may vary.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false: defects per unit is a u chart. 2 false: limits come from p̄ and n, never specifications. 3 true: n may vary. 4 true: a defective is a whole unit, counted once."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"3 and 4 are both true."}],final:"3 and 4 true → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false: defects per unit is u-chart data, and specifications never set control limits.","Incorrect — includes statement 2; p-chart limits come from process data only.","","Incorrect — includes statement 1, which describes the u chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-973",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The p chart monitors the [?] of defective units in each subgroup.`,options:["number","proportion","range","variance"],answer:1,hint:"p stands for this.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The p chart plots the **proportion** defective: defectives divided by the subgroup size."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The number defective is the np chart; range and variance describe continuous data."}],final:"p = proportion defective → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the number of defective units is plotted on an np chart.","","Incorrect — ranges are plotted on R charts for continuous data.","Incorrect — variance describes the spread of continuous data, not attribute counts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-974",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

When a p chart shows a special cause, the team refers to the [?] to decide which root causes to investigate and what actions to take.`,options:["charter","FMEA","OCAP","SIPOC"],answer:2,hint:"This plan is written in advance for out-of-control conditions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's example refers to the **OCAP** (out-of-control action plan): which causes to check and which actions bring the process back into control."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The charter sets project scope; FMEA ranks potential failure modes; SIPOC scopes the process."}],final:"Signal → OCAP → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charter defines the project's problem, goal and scope, not the response to a signal.","Incorrect — FMEA identifies and ranks risks; it is not the response guide for a chart signal.","","Incorrect — SIPOC is a high-level scoping map, not an action plan for signals."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547–548"},{id:"sv-5-975",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a characteristic of the p chart?",options:["Its limits change when the subgroup size changes","It requires the same number of units in every subgroup","It classes each unit as defective or not","It is based on the binomial distribution"],answer:1,hint:"Three options are true of the p chart; one belongs to its neighbour.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The p chart is binomial, treats units as defective or not, and its limits move with n."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"A **constant** sample size is the requirement of the **np** chart, not the p chart."}],final:"Constant n is an np-chart requirement → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p-chart limits depend on each subgroup's n, so they change with it.","","Incorrect — the p chart tracks defectives, so each unit is either defective or not.","Incorrect — the p chart is built on the binomial distribution."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-976",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of these should NOT be drawn on a p chart?",options:["The upper control limit for each subgroup","The centre line p̄","The lower control limit for each subgroup","The customer's maximum acceptable proportion defective"],answer:3,hint:"Control charts show the voice of the process only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A p chart shows the centre line and the control limits, all calculated from process data."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"A customer maximum is a **specification**; the book never draws specifications on control charts."}],final:"Customer limit is a specification → not drawn → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — each subgroup's UCL is part of the chart.","Incorrect — p̄ is the centre line of every p chart.","Incorrect — each subgroup's LCL (or zero, if negative) is part of the chart.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548; Book p. 524"},{id:"sv-5-977",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A foundry inspects every casting made on each shift and records whether it is scrapped. Production varies from 150 to 400 castings a shift. Which control chart should be used?",options:["P chart","C chart","NP chart","U chart"],answer:0,hint:"Scrapped or not, with a varying number made.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each casting is **defective or not** (scrapped), and the number inspected **varies** → **p chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"np needs a constant sample; u and c count defects, not scrapped units."}],final:"Defectives + varying n → P chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a c chart counts defects in a constant amount inspected.","Incorrect — an np chart needs the same number of castings each shift.","Incorrect — a u chart counts defects per unit, not scrapped castings."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-978",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A dental clinic records each day how many patients arrived late for their appointments. The number of appointments differs from day to day. Which chart should the practice manager use?",options:["NP chart","C chart","P chart","U chart"],answer:2,hint:"Each patient is either late or on time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"This is one of the book's p-chart examples: each patient is late or not (a **defective**), and the number of appointments **varies**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"u and c count defects; np needs a constant number of appointments."}],final:"Late or not, varying n → P chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an np chart needs the same number of appointments every day.","Incorrect — a c chart counts defects in a constant amount inspected.","","Incorrect — a u chart counts defects per unit; a patient is simply late or not."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-979",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Auditors check a varying number of supplier invoices each day. An invoice can contain several errors, but the finance director cares only whether an invoice must be reissued, which any error forces. Which chart best matches the director's question?",options:["U chart","P chart","NP chart","C chart"],answer:1,hint:"Decide what the 'bad' outcome is: an error or a reissued invoice?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The director's measure is **reissue or not** for each invoice — a **defective**. The number checked **varies** → **p chart**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The fact that invoices can hold several errors is a trap; it would matter only if errors per invoice were the measure (then u)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"u and c count defects; np needs a constant daily sample."}],final:"Reissue or not, varying n → P chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a u chart would track errors per invoice, which is not the director's question.","","Incorrect — an np chart needs the same number of invoices checked every day.","Incorrect — a c chart counts defects and needs a constant amount inspected."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-980",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team plans a p chart of defective forms. The process runs at about 2% defective, and the team proposes daily samples of 10 forms. What is the main concern with this plan?",options:["The samples are too large and will hide special causes","Ten forms is ideal, because p charts use subgroups of 2–5","The samples are too small; the book says p-chart samples are usually larger than 50","The sample size must equal the number of days charted"],answer:2,hint:"How many defectives will a sample of 10 usually contain at a 2% rate?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With about 2% defective, most samples of 10 contain **no** defectives, so the chart can hardly signal anything. The book's selection table says p-chart samples are usually **larger than 50**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Larger samples make the chart more sensitive, not less; subgroups of 2–5 are for Xbar-R; the number of days has nothing to do with n."}],final:"Samples too small for a 2% rate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — larger samples give narrower limits and a more sensitive chart.","Incorrect — subgroups of 2–5 are the rule for Xbar-R charts of continuous data.","","Incorrect — the sample size per day and the number of days charted are unrelated."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-981",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"After a p-chart signal, the team took corrective action. The next week's proportion is back inside the limits but still above the centre line. What is the BEST action?",options:["Make no further changes, because the process is back in control","Stop charting until several weeks fall below the centre line","Recalculate the limits using the corrected week","Adjust the process again to bring the proportion below the centre line"],answer:0,hint:"What does the book say once the process is back in control?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: after the corrective action, wait for the next sample; if it is back in control, **make no more changes** — even if it is above the centre line."},{title:"Apply",body:"Points scatter around p̄ by common cause. Adjusting again would be tampering."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculation needs a documented lasting change; stopping the chart removes monitoring."}],final:"Back in control → no further changes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — monitoring must continue; the chart is what shows whether the process stays in control.","Incorrect — one week of data is no basis for new limits; recalculation follows documented process changes.","Incorrect — chasing points above the centre line is tampering with a stable process."],bookRef:"Book p. 548"},{id:"sv-5-982",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"To monitor three new weeks on an existing p chart, the Green Belt added them to the data and let Minitab recalculate the limits each time. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because the limits should always include all available data","Yes, because recalculating keeps the chart up to date","No, because p charts may only be used for the original 20 weeks","No, because the historical p̄ should be used so that new weeks are judged against fixed limits"],answer:3,hint:"Monitoring needs fixed limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In the book's example the historical p̄ is entered so that Minitab does **not** recalculate the limits as new data arrive."},{title:"Apply",body:"Recalculating each time lets new points move the limits, which can hide a special cause."}],final:"Freeze the limits → No → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — including every new point lets a special cause shift the limits and hide itself.","Incorrect — 'up to date' limits are not the aim; monitoring compares new points with the baseline.","Incorrect — p charts are used for ongoing monitoring; 20 weeks is a minimum for setting limits, not a maximum.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 547–548"},{id:"sv-5-983",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`The book's paycheck-rework p chart is extended with three new weeks:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Reworked Paychecks
Subgroup size n = 250    P-bar = 0.2038 (historical)
UCL = 0.2802   LCL = 0.1274

Week   Defectives     P
 21        61       0.244
 22        64       0.256
 23        77       0.308

Test Results for P Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 23
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The process is capable, because two of the three weeks are below the 25% goal","Weeks 21 and 22 are also special causes, because they are above p̄","The limits should be recalculated to include weeks 21 to 23","Week 23 shows a special cause, so the team should follow the OCAP"],answer:3,hint:"Compare each week with the UCL, not with the centre line.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Weeks 21 (0.244) and 22 (0.256) are inside the limits. Week 23 (0.308) is above the UCL of 0.2802 — Test 1."},{title:"Decide",body:"Week 23 is a special cause, and the book's team refers to the **OCAP**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Points above p̄ but inside the limits are common cause; a goal is not a capability verdict; limits stay frozen during monitoring."}],final:"Week 23 beyond UCL → OCAP → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability is judged against requirements over time, not from two weeks; the signal still needs action.","Incorrect — points above the centre line but inside the limits are common-cause variation.","Incorrect — monitoring limits stay frozen; recalculation follows documented process changes.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 546–548"},{id:"sv-5-984",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A p chart of defective welds prints:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Defective Welds
Subgroup size n = 200    P-bar = 0.100
UCL = 0.164   LCL = 0.036

Test Results for P Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 4, 17     (P = 0.025, 0.020; both below LCL)

Note: points 4 and 17 were inspected by a temporary inspector.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Days 4 and 17 prove that the welding process has improved","The inspection on days 4 and 17 should be checked before any improvement is claimed","The LCL should be set to zero so that low points no longer signal","Points below the LCL are good news and need no investigation"],answer:1,hint:"A low signal can come from the process or from the measurement.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Both signals are **below** the LCL, and both days used a temporary inspector."},{title:"Interpret",body:"A point below the LCL may be a real improvement — or defectives the inspector missed. The shared inspector points to the **measurement**, so it is checked first."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Claiming proof ignores the inspector; ignoring low signals misses problems; moving the LCL hides them."}],final:"Low signals + new inspector → check inspection → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the shared temporary inspector makes missed defectives a likely cause; improvement is not proven.","","Incorrect — the LCL comes from the data; setting it to zero by hand would hide genuine signals.","Incorrect — a signal in either direction is a special cause to investigate."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 539, 544–548"},{id:"sv-5-985",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A p chart of late deliveries prints:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Late Deliveries
P-bar = 0.120
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes
Test Results for P Chart: No tests failed.

Customer requirement: no more than 5% late
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The process is stable but averages about 12% late, so an improvement project is needed to meet 5%","The control limits should be reset to 5% so that late weeks show as signals","The process is unstable, because its average is above the customer requirement","The process meets the requirement, because no tests failed"],answer:0,hint:"Stable and capable are different questions.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"No tests failed → the process is **stable** (only common-cause variation)."},{title:"Interpret",body:"The book says the p chart's centre line is also the **process capability** for the attribute: about 12% late, well above the 5% requirement."},{title:"Decide",body:"A stable but unacceptable process needs a change to the system (an improvement project), not reaction to individual points."}],final:"Stable but not capable → improve the system → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — requirements are never used as control limits; that would turn common-cause weeks into false signals.","Incorrect — stability is judged by the tests, and none failed; being above a requirement is a capability problem.","Incorrect — 'no tests failed' means stable, not that 12% late meets a 5% requirement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"},{id:"sv-5-986",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A p chart of incomplete orders is split into two stages:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Incomplete Orders, by Stage
Stage 1 (weeks 1-20):  P-bar = 0.150   Test results: no tests failed
Stage 2 (weeks 21-40): P-bar = 0.080   Test results: no tests failed

Note: a redesigned order form was introduced and documented at week 21.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Stage 2 should be judged against the stage 1 limits for the rest of the project","Stage 2 is out of control, because its p̄ differs from the stage 1 p̄","The new form gave a lower, stable proportion, and stage 2 limits reflect the documented change","The improvement cannot be judged, because the limits changed at week 21"],answer:2,hint:"When is it right to recalculate limits?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Both stages pass all tests, and p̄ falls from 0.150 to 0.080 at the documented change in week 21."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Limits are recalculated after a **documented process change**. Stage 2 has its own limits, and its stability shows the new, lower level is sustained."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A different p̄ between stages is the point of staging, not a signal; old limits no longer describe the changed process."}],final:"Documented change → new stable stage at a lower p̄ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the stage 1 limits describe the old process; the book recalculates after documented changes.","Incorrect — each stage is judged against its own limits, and stage 2 shows no failed tests.","","Incorrect — staging the chart at a documented change is exactly how an improvement is shown."],bookRef:"Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-987",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the book's response to a p-chart signal in order:

- **P** — Refer to the OCAP to identify likely root causes and actions
- **Q** — A new point falls above the UCL
- **R** — Take the corrective action and wait for the next sample
- **S** — Make no further changes once the next sample is back in control`,options:["Q → R → P → S","P → Q → R → S","Q → P → S → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:3,hint:"Signal, plan, act, then stop adjusting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's sequence: a signal appears (Q), the team consults the **OCAP** (P), takes the action and waits for the next sample (R), and if it is back in control **makes no more changes** (S)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Acting before consulting the OCAP, or consulting it before any signal, breaks the logic."}],final:"Signal → OCAP → act and wait → no more changes → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — acts before consulting the OCAP, so the action is not guided by the plan.","Incorrect — the OCAP is used in response to a signal, so the signal comes first.","Incorrect — 'no further changes' can only be decided after the corrective action and the next sample.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 547–548"},{id:"sv-5-988",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does an np chart plot for each subgroup?",options:["The number of defective units in a sample of constant size","The proportion of defective units in samples of varying size","The number of defects in a sample of constant size","The average number of defects per unit inspected"],answer:0,hint:"n × p: a count of bad units.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart plots the **number of defective units** in each subgroup, and the subgroup size is **constant**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Proportion with varying n is the p chart; a count of defects is the c chart; defects per unit is the u chart."}],final:"np = count of defectives, constant n → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a proportion defective with varying sample sizes is plotted on a p chart.","Incorrect — a count of defects in a constant sample is plotted on a c chart.","Incorrect — defects per unit are plotted on a u chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-989",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which condition must hold before an np chart can be used?",options:["The data are continuous measurements","Each unit can have several defects counted","Every subgroup has the same sample size","Each subgroup contains 2–5 units"],answer:2,hint:"The np chart plots raw counts, so the samples must be comparable.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart compares **counts** of defectives, which only makes sense if every sample has the **same size**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Several defects per unit is u/c data; continuous data and subgroups of 2–5 describe the Xbar-R chart."}],final:"Constant sample size → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — continuous measurements need a variables chart such as Xbar-R or I-MR.","Incorrect — counting several defects per unit is defect data for c and u charts.","","Incorrect — subgroups of 2–5 are the rule for Xbar-R charts; np samples are usually larger than 50."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-990",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why must the sample size be constant on an np chart?",options:["A constant sample lets the chart count defects as well as defectives","The binomial distribution cannot be used when sample sizes vary","Minitab cannot calculate limits for more than one sample size","A count of defectives can only be compared fairly between samples of the same size"],answer:3,hint:"Is 10 defectives out of 100 the same as 10 out of 500?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The same count means different things in different-sized samples: 10 out of 100 is 10%, 10 out of 500 is 2%. With a **fixed n** counts are directly comparable, and the centre line and limits stay put."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The p chart is also binomial and handles varying n; Minitab draws p charts with varying n; no defectives chart counts defects."}],final:"Counts comparable only with fixed n → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — np charts count defective units only; defects are counted on c and u charts.","Incorrect — the p chart is binomial too and works with varying sample sizes.","Incorrect — Minitab handles varying sizes on p charts; the issue is that raw counts are not comparable.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-991",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A team checks a fixed sample every day. Why might it choose an np chart rather than a p chart?",options:["The np chart can also count several defects on each unit","The counts are easier for operators to read, and the signals are the same","The np chart detects smaller shifts than the p chart","The np chart does not need a centre line"],answer:1,hint:"With constant n, the np limits are just the p limits multiplied by n.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With a constant n, the np chart is the p chart scaled by n, so both give **the same signals**. Many operators find a **count** easier to read than a proportion."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Neither chart is more sensitive on the same data; both have a centre line; neither counts defects."}],final:"Same signals, easier counts → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — np charts count defective units; several defects per unit belong on a c or u chart.","","Incorrect — on constant-n data the two charts give exactly the same signals.","Incorrect — the np chart has a centre line, np̄, like every control chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-992",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does an np chart show that a Pareto chart does not?",options:["Which defect categories occur most often","The cumulative percentage of defects by category","The root cause of each defective unit","Whether the number of defectives is stable over time"],answer:3,hint:"One tool is time-ordered; the other ranks categories.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An np chart plots defective counts **in time order** with control limits, so it shows **stability**. A Pareto chart ranks **categories** to find the vital few."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Most frequent categories and the cumulative line are Pareto features; neither tool shows root causes."}],final:"np chart = stability over time → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ranking categories to find the vital few is the job of a Pareto chart.","Incorrect — the cumulative percentage line is part of a Pareto chart.","Incorrect — neither chart explains why a unit failed; root causes need analysis tools.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540; Book p. 523"},{id:"sv-5-993",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How is the centre line of an np chart obtained?",options:["Total defectives divided by the number of subgroups","Total defects divided by the total units inspected","The customer's maximum allowed number of defectives","Total defectives divided by the total units inspected"],answer:0,hint:"The centre line is in the same units as the plotted points: a count per sample.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book gives the np centre line as **total defectives ÷ number of subgroups** — the average count per sample."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Total defectives ÷ total units is p̄ (a proportion); total defects ÷ total units is ū; customer limits are never centre lines."}],final:"np̄ = total defectives ÷ number of subgroups → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — that gives ū, the u-chart centre line for defects per unit.","Incorrect — a customer maximum is a specification and never a centre line.","Incorrect — that gives p̄, the average proportion defective, not the average count."],trap:"Total ÷ total units gives p̄, not np̄.",bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-994",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"On an np chart, six consecutive counts each rise above the one before, all inside the limits. How should this be treated?",options:["As a signal only if the next count exceeds the UCL","As a signal of a special cause, because the trend test applies to np charts","As common-cause variation, because run tests apply only to Xbar charts","As common-cause variation, because no point is outside the limits"],answer:1,hint:"Attribute charts use tests 1–4.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Tests 1–4 apply to attribute charts. Test 3 is **6 points in a row all increasing or all decreasing**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Six rising counts fail Test 3, so this is a special-cause signal even though every point is inside the limits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Signals are not limited to points beyond the limits, and trend tests are not limited to Xbar charts."}],final:"6 rising points → Test 3 signal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the trend is already a signal; there is no need to wait for a point beyond the UCL.","","Incorrect — tests 1–4, including the trend test, are applied to attribute charts such as np.","Incorrect — run and trend tests detect special causes even when every point is inside the limits."],bookRef:"Book p. 532"},{id:"sv-5-995",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"An np chart and a c chart both need a constant sample size. What distinguishes them?",options:["The np chart is for varying sample sizes, while the c chart is for constant sizes","The np chart is for continuous data, while the c chart is for counts","The np chart counts defective units, while the c chart counts defects","The np chart counts defects, while the c chart counts defective units"],answer:2,hint:"Defectives versus defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Both need a constant n. **np** counts **defective units** (binomial); **c** counts **defects** (Poisson), several of which can sit on one unit."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"One option swaps the roles; neither chart handles varying n; both are attribute charts."}],final:"np = defectives, c = defects → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both charts need a constant sample size; p and u handle varying sizes.","Incorrect — both are attribute charts for counts; continuous data use variables charts.","","Incorrect — this swaps the two charts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-996",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which word pair separates the data on np and p charts from the data on c and u charts?",options:["Proportions versus counts","Defectives versus defects","Constant versus variable sample size","Continuous versus discrete"],answer:1,hint:"One family judges whole units; the other counts flaws.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"np and p charts track **defectives** (whole units good or bad). c and u charts track **defects** (flaws, several per unit)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Each family has a count chart and a rate chart (np/p, c/u), and each has a constant-n and a variable-n chart; all four are discrete."}],final:"Defectives vs defects → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — np and c are counts while p and u are rates, so this pair does not separate the families.","","Incorrect — np and c need constant n, p and u allow variable n, so this cuts across both families.","Incorrect — all four attribute charts use discrete data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-997",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the np chart is CORRECT?",options:["Its limits are straight lines, because the sample size is constant","It counts every defect on a unit separately","It is based on the Poisson distribution","Its limits step up and down with the sample size"],answer:0,hint:"What do constant n and binomial data imply?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With a **constant n**, the np centre line and limits are the same for every subgroup, so they are **straight lines**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Stepped limits come from varying n (p, u); counting each defect is c/u data; np is binomial, not Poisson."}],final:"Constant n → straight limits → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — np counts each defective unit once; defects are counted on c and u charts.","Incorrect — the np chart is binomial; Poisson underlies c and u charts.","Incorrect — stepped limits belong to p and u charts with varying sample sizes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-998",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"For the same constant sample size, how are the np-chart limits related to the p-chart limits?",options:["They are unrelated, because np charts use the Poisson distribution","They are the p-chart limits divided by the sample size","They are the same as the c-chart limits for that sample size","They are the p-chart limits multiplied by the sample size"],answer:3,hint:"np = n × p, so scale the p chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that NP limits are simply the **P-chart limits multiplied by n**. That is why both charts give the same signals on constant-n data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c-chart limits omit the (1 − p̄) term because they model defects; dividing by n goes the wrong way; both charts are binomial."}],final:"NP limits = P limits × n → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — np and p charts are both binomial; Poisson underlies c and u charts.","Incorrect — dividing would give a proportion smaller than p; the np count is n times the proportion.","Incorrect — c-chart limits are based on the Poisson model for defects and lack the (1 − p̄) term.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-999",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the sample size for an np chart is CORRECT?",options:["Subgroups of 2–5 units are recommended","It may vary freely as long as every sample has more than 50 units","The book's table says it is usually constant and greater than 50","One unit per subgroup is enough when testing is destructive"],answer:2,hint:"Defectives are rare, so samples must be fairly large — and fixed.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's selection table: the np chart's sample size is **usually constant** and **usually greater than 50**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"2–5 is the Xbar-R rule; a varying size calls for a p chart; one unit per subgroup describes I-MR."}],final:"Constant, usually > 50 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — subgroups of 2–5 are the rule for Xbar-R charts of continuous data.","Incorrect — if the sample size varies, the book switches to a p chart.","","Incorrect — a subgroup of one is the I-MR case for continuous data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-1000",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the np chart are CORRECT?

1. It needs a constant sample size.
2. It can count several defects on the same unit.
3. It is the right chart for scratches per panel.
4. It plots a count of defective units.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Count of defectives, fixed n.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 true: n must be constant. 2 false: each unit is counted once as defective. 3 false: scratches are defects (c or u chart). 4 true: the y-axis is the count of defective units."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"1 and 4 are both true."}],final:"1 and 4 true → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 2; several defects per unit are counted on c or u charts.","Incorrect — both are false: np counts defective units, and scratches are defects.","Incorrect — includes statement 3; scratches per panel are defects for a c or u chart.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1001",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing letter in the following sentence.

If the sample size of an np chart starts to vary, the team should switch to a [?] chart.`,options:["c","np","p","u"],answer:2,hint:"Which chart tracks defectives with a varying sample size?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defectives with a **varying** sample size → **p chart**. The book: the p chart can do everything the np chart can."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c and u count defects; np is the chart that no longer fits."}],final:"Varying n with defectives → p → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a c chart counts defects and also needs a constant sample size.","Incorrect — np is the current chart, which needs a constant sample size.","","Incorrect — a u chart handles varying sizes but counts defects, not defective units."],bookRef:"Book p. 521"},{id:"sv-5-1002",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

An np chart counts [?] units, so a unit with three faults is counted only once.`,options:["conforming","defective","inspected","sampled"],answer:1,hint:"Which word describes a whole unit that fails?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart counts **defective** units: a unit either fails or not, however many faults it has."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Conforming units are the good ones; inspected and sampled describe the whole sample, which is fixed at n."}],final:"np counts defective units → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — conforming units are the good ones; the chart counts the units that fail.","","Incorrect — the number inspected is the constant sample size n, not the plotted count.","Incorrect — the number sampled is n itself, which is fixed on an np chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1003",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which situation is NOT suited to an np chart?",options:["Rejected castings in a fixed sample of 100 per shift","Invoices with any error in a fixed audit of 150 per day","Late orders each day, with daily order volume varying from 80 to 400","Failed SIM cards in a fixed sample of 200 per lot"],answer:2,hint:"Three samples are fixed; one is not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart needs defective units in a **constant** sample."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"Daily order volume **varies** from 80 to 400, so raw counts of late orders are not comparable → **p chart**."}],final:"Varying volume → p chart, not np → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — rejected castings in a fixed 100 per shift fit an np chart.","Incorrect — an invoice with any error is a defective, and the audit size is fixed: np chart.","","Incorrect — failed cards in a fixed sample of 200 are defectives with constant n: np chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1004",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"A team uses an np chart of rejected castings. Which statement about the chart is NOT correct?",options:["The same sample size is used on every shift","A count above the UCL suggests a special cause","The centre line is the average number of rejected castings per sample","A casting with two cracks is counted as two rejected castings"],answer:3,hint:"Defective units are counted once.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An np chart counts **defective units**. A casting is rejected or not, so a casting with two cracks is **one** rejected casting."},{title:"Check the others",body:"np̄ is the average count per sample; a point above the UCL is Test 1; a constant n is required."}],final:"Two cracks = one rejected casting → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is true: an np chart needs a constant sample size.","Incorrect — this is true: a point beyond 3-sigma limits is a special-cause signal.","Incorrect — this is true: np̄ is total defectives divided by the number of subgroups.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1005",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An electronics plant tests exactly 120 circuit boards from each shift and records how many fail the functional test. Which control chart should be used?",options:["U chart","NP chart","C chart","P chart"],answer:1,hint:"Failed or not, with a fixed sample.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each board **passes or fails** (defective), and the sample is **fixed** at 120 → **np chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A p chart would also work, but np is the book's choice for counts with constant n; c and u count defects."}],final:"Defectives + constant n → NP chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a u chart counts defects per unit with varying sample sizes.","","Incorrect — a c chart counts defects, not failed boards.","Incorrect — a p chart is meant for varying sample sizes; with a fixed 120 the np chart shows simple counts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1006",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A packaging line samples 100 cartons every hour and counts the print defects found on them. One carton can have several print defects. Which control chart should be used?",options:["C chart","P chart","U chart","NP chart"],answer:0,hint:"Constant sample, but are you counting bad cartons or defects?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Print defects are **defects** (several per carton), and the sample is **constant** at 100 → **c chart**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The fixed sample makes np tempting, but np counts defective cartons, not defects."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"p is for proportion defective; u would work but is meant for varying sample sizes."}],final:"Defects + constant n → C chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a p chart tracks the proportion of defective cartons, ignoring the defect count.","Incorrect — a u chart is meant for defects when the sample size varies; here it is fixed at 100.","Incorrect — an np chart counts defective cartons, not the number of print defects."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540; Book p. 520"},{id:"sv-5-1007",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt wants to monitor whether the number of defective welds in a fixed sample of 80 per shift is stable, and also to find which weld fault types cause most rejects. Which pair of tools fits?",options:["A u chart for stability and an FMEA for the fault types","A c chart for stability and a histogram for the fault types","A Pareto chart for stability and an np chart for the fault types","An np chart for stability and a Pareto chart for the fault types"],answer:3,hint:"One tool tracks time; the other ranks categories.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defective welds in a **fixed** sample over time → **np chart** for stability. Ranking fault types to find the vital few → **Pareto chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The pairing is reversed in one option; c and u charts count defects; a histogram shows the shape of continuous data; FMEA ranks risks, not observed frequencies."}],final:"np chart + Pareto chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a u chart counts defects per unit, and an FMEA ranks potential risks, not observed fault frequencies.","Incorrect — a c chart counts defects, and a histogram shows the distribution of continuous data.","Incorrect — this swaps the tools; a Pareto chart ranks categories and an np chart tracks stability.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540; Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-5-1008",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"An np chart of failed seals (n = 100 per shift) shows one count above the UCL on a night shift. The supervisor wants to reset the machine settings at the start of every shift 'to be safe'. What is the BEST action?",options:["Recalculate the limits to include the night-shift point","Increase the sample to 200 so that the point falls inside the limits","Investigate the night-shift signal and act on its cause, leaving the settings alone when there is no signal","Reset the settings every shift as proposed, since it can only reduce failures"],answer:2,hint:"React to signals, not to every shift.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A point above the UCL is a special cause to **investigate**. Without a signal, the process is left alone."},{title:"Apply",body:"Resetting settings every shift is **tampering**: it adds variation to a stable process."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculating hides the signal; changing n to make a point 'fit' changes the chart, not the cause."}],final:"Investigate the signal; no routine resets → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — recalculating the limits to include the signal hides the special cause.","Incorrect — changing the sample size to absorb a point does nothing about the cause of the failures.","","Incorrect — adjusting a stable process without a signal is tampering and adds variation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540; Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-5-1009",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A warehouse audits a fixed 60 picked orders a day and counts the orders with any picking error. The Green Belt chose a c chart because the sample size is constant. Was this an appropriate choice?",options:["Yes, because a c chart needs a constant sample size","Yes, because picking errors are counts","No, because daily data must be plotted on an I-MR chart","No, because the data are defective orders, so an np chart fits"],answer:3,hint:"Is the team counting errors, or orders with errors?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"'Orders with any error' classes each order as **defective or not**. With a fixed 60 a day, that is **np-chart** data."},{title:"Apply",body:"A c chart counts **defects** (all errors), which is not what is recorded here."}],final:"Defective orders, fixed n → np chart → No → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a constant sample size is needed, but the c chart also assumes defects are counted, not defective orders.","Incorrect — the count is of orders with errors (defectives), not of the errors themselves.","Incorrect — attribute counts with a fixed sample suit an np chart; I-MR is for continuous individual values.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1010",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
NP Chart of Failed Boards
Subgroup size n = 100     NP-bar = 5.00
UCL = 11.54   LCL = 0

Test Results for NP Chart of Failed Boards
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points: 17     (NP = 13)
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Shift 17 had 13 defects on each board","Shift 17 had 13 failed boards out of 100, a special cause to investigate","Shift 17 had a failure rate of 13%, which is within the control limits","The LCL of 0 shows the chart is not valid for these data"],answer:1,hint:"The np chart plots a count of defective units per sample.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"NP = 13 is a count of failed boards in a sample of 100, and it is above the UCL of 11.54 → Test 1."},{title:"Decide",body:"Shift 17 is a special-cause signal to investigate."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The count is above the UCL; it is not defects per board; an LCL of 0 just means the calculated LCL was negative."}],final:"13 failed boards > UCL → special cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the np chart counts defective boards, not defects on each board.","","Incorrect — 13 failed boards is above the UCL of 11.54, so it is outside the limits, not within them.","Incorrect — an LCL of 0 means the calculated lower limit was negative and was set to zero; the chart is valid."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1011",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
NP Chart of Rejected Moulded Caps
Subgroup size n = 150     NP-bar = 12.00
UCL = 21.97   LCL = 2.03

Test Results for NP Chart
TEST 4. 14 points in a row alternating up and down.
Test Failed at points: 30

Note: samples alternate between the day shift and the night shift.
\`\`\`

What does this MOST likely indicate?`,options:["The two shifts differ systematically, so the data should be split by shift and the difference investigated","There is no signal, because every point is inside the control limits","The sample size is too small, so the chart should be replaced with a p chart","The process mean has drifted upward over the last 14 samples"],answer:0,hint:"An up-down zigzag often means two sources taking turns.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Test 4 (14 points alternating up and down) failed, and the samples alternate between two shifts."},{title:"Interpret",body:"A regular zigzag is a non-random pattern — typically two streams (here shifts) with different reject levels plotted in turn. Splitting the data by shift shows the difference."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Run tests signal inside the limits; alternation is not a drift; n = 150 is fixed and well above 50."}],final:"Test 4 + alternating shifts → two streams → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Test 4 detects non-random patterns even when every point is inside the limits.","Incorrect — the sample size is fixed at 150, above the usual 50; a p chart would show the same pattern.","Incorrect — a drift would show as a run or trend (Tests 2 or 3), not an alternating zigzag."],bookRef:"Book p. 532; Book p. 517"},{id:"sv-5-1012",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
NP Chart of Defective Moulded Parts
Subgroup size n = 200     NP-bar = 12.00
UCL = 22.08   LCL = 1.92

Test Results for NP Chart
TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points: 30, 31, 32
(points 22-32 all below the center line)

Note: a new resin supplier has been used since lot 22.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["There is no signal, because no point is below the LCL","The number of defectives has most likely fallen since the supplier change, which should be confirmed and documented","The limits must be recalculated immediately using lots 22 to 32 only","The process has become unstable and the new supplier should be dropped"],answer:1,hint:"A long run on one side of the centre line is a shift.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Test 2 failed: every lot since 22 is below the centre line, and lot 22 is when the new supplier started."},{title:"Interpret",body:"This is a sustained **downward shift** in defectives — a special cause, apparently a favourable one. Confirm the cause and document it."},{title:"Next step",body:"Once the change is confirmed as permanent and documented, the limits can be recalculated for the new level."}],final:"Test 2 below CL → favourable shift to confirm → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 9 points in a row on one side of the centre line is a signal even without a point beyond a limit.","","Incorrect — recalculation follows confirmation and documentation of the change, not the first sign of a shift.","Incorrect — fewer defectives is an improvement; dropping the supplier would reverse it."],bookRef:"Book p. 532; Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-1013",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for choosing an attribute chart for rejected units in order:

- **P** — Decide whether each unit is classed as defective or its defects are counted
- **Q** — Check whether the sample size is constant
- **R** — Select the np chart
- **S** — Collect enough baseline subgroups to calculate the limits`,options:["Q → P → S → R","P → R → Q → S","S → P → Q → R","P → Q → R → S"],answer:3,hint:"Data type first, then sample size, then the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Chart choice follows **data type → subgroup size**: first defective vs defects (P), then constant vs varying n (Q). Only then is the np chart chosen (R)."},{title:"Then",body:"Baseline subgroups are collected to set the limits (S)."}],final:"Data type → sample size → chart → baseline → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — checks the sample size before knowing whether the data are defectives or defects, and collects data before choosing the chart.","Incorrect — selects the np chart before confirming that the sample size is constant.","Incorrect — collects baseline data before deciding what is being measured and charted.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-5-1014",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put the steps for setting up and using an np chart in order:

- **P** — Calculate the centre line and limits from the baseline subgroups
- **Q** — Fix the sample size and the sampling interval
- **R** — Plot new counts against the frozen limits
- **S** — Collect baseline subgroups of defective counts`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → P → Q → R","Q → P → S → R","S → Q → P → R"],answer:0,hint:"The sample size must be fixed before any data are collected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart needs a **constant n**, so the sampling plan comes first (Q)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Baseline counts are collected (S), the limits are calculated from them (P), and new counts are then monitored against those frozen limits (R)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Collecting data before fixing n gives counts that cannot be compared, and limits cannot exist before the baseline data."}],final:"Fix n → baseline → limits → monitor → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — collects data and calculates limits before fixing the sample size.","Incorrect — limits cannot be calculated before the baseline subgroups are collected.","Incorrect — collects baseline data before the sample size is fixed, so the counts may not be comparable."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1015",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The np chart is based on the binomial distribution.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Each unit is either defective or not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The np chart counts defective units in a fixed sample. Each unit has two outcomes, so the count follows the **binomial** distribution — the (1 − p̄) term in its limits comes from it."},{title:"Apply",body:"The statement is true; the Poisson distribution belongs to c and u charts."}],final:"Defective or not → binomial, so the statement is True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the Poisson distribution is used for defects (c, u); np counts defectives, which are binomial."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 530–531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-1016",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A bottling plant installs a checkweigher that records the fill weight of every bottle. The team forms a subgroup from 20 consecutive bottles every 10 minutes. Which control chart should be used for fill weight?",options:["NP chart","Xbar-R chart","Xbar-S chart","I-MR chart"],answer:2,hint:"Continuous data, subgroups of 20: which spread measure uses all the values?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Fill weight is continuous. With subgroups of **10 or more**, the book selects **Xbar-S**."},{title:"Apply",body:"With 20 values per subgroup the range would use only the largest and smallest bottle; the standard deviation uses all 20."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R is for 2–5, I-MR for single values, NP for counts of defective units."}],final:"Continuous data, n = 20 → Xbar-S → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an NP chart counts defective units; fill weight is continuous data.","Incorrect — Xbar-R suits subgroups of 2–5; with 20 values the range ignores most of the data.","","Incorrect — I-MR is for individual values (a subgroup size of one)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1017",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A new holding fixture was fitted before subgroup 31 on a machining line.

\`\`\`
Xbar-S Chart of Bore Depth (mm x 0.01)    n = 10
Xbar Chart:  UCL = 51.95   CL = 50.00   LCL = 48.05
S Chart:     UCL = 3.43    CL = 2.00    LCL = 0.57

Test Results for S Chart
TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points: 39, 40
(points 31-40 all below the center line)

Test Results for Xbar Chart: No test failures.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["There is no signal, because no point on the S chart is outside its control limits","Within-subgroup variation has most likely decreased since the fixture change, a special cause to confirm","The process mean has most likely shifted, because a test has failed after the fixture change","The S-chart result can be ignored, because run tests are not applied to S charts"],answer:1,hint:"Which chart failed, and what does that chart measure?",steps:[{title:"Locate the signal",body:"Test 2 (9 points in a row on one side) failed on the **S chart**, with every point since the change below the centre line."},{title:"Interpret",body:"The S chart monitors **within-subgroup spread**, so the spread has most likely dropped — a special cause (here a welcome one) to confirm and document."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Xbar chart shows no failures, so there is no evidence of a mean shift; Test 2 is one of the four tests used on S charts."}],final:"Test 2 on the S chart → spread has shifted down → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a run of 9 points on one side is a signal (Test 2) even when every point is inside the limits.","","Incorrect — the test failed on the S chart, which measures spread; the Xbar chart (level) shows no failures.","Incorrect — tests 1–4 are applied to the S chart, and Test 2 is one of them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539; Book p. 532"},{id:"sv-5-1018",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about measuring within-subgroup spread are CORRECT?

1. The range uses only the largest and smallest values in a subgroup.
2. The standard deviation uses every value in a subgroup.
3. The range becomes more efficient than the standard deviation as the subgroup size grows.
4. An Xbar-S chart does not need rational subgroups.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Which spread measure throws information away in a large subgroup?",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is true: R = largest − smallest. 2 is true: S is calculated from all values. 3 is false: R wastes more information as n grows, which is why the book switches to S at 10+. 4 is false: both Xbar-R and Xbar-S need rational subgroups."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"Only 1 and 2 are both true."}],final:"1 and 2 true → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes statement 3, which reverses the truth: R becomes less efficient as the subgroup grows.","Incorrect — both statements are false: R loses efficiency at large n, and every Shewhart chart needs rational subgroups.","Incorrect — includes statement 4; Xbar-S charts need rational subgroups just as Xbar-R charts do."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1019",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book recommend an S chart instead of an R chart when outliers are a concern?",options:["The standard deviation ignores the largest value in each subgroup","The S chart uses specification limits, so outliers are judged against the tolerance","The range is set entirely by the two extreme values, so one outlier can distort it","The S chart removes outliers automatically before the points are plotted"],answer:2,hint:"How many values in a subgroup decide the range?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The range is the largest minus the smallest value. One extreme reading becomes one end of the range, so R jumps."},{title:"Apply",body:"The standard deviation is built from every value, so one outlier is diluted by the rest; the book notes outliers cause issues with range charts."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing is removed automatically, S uses all values, and control charts never use specification limits."}],final:"R depends only on the extremes → S preferred with outliers → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the standard deviation uses every value, including the largest.","Incorrect — S-chart limits come from s̄ and the B constants; specifications are never used for control limits.","","Incorrect — no chart deletes data; the S statistic is simply less dominated by a single extreme value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1020",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

In an Xbar-S pair, the Xbar chart monitors the process [?] from one subgroup to the next.`,options:["capability","level","spread","tolerance"],answer:1,hint:"One chart of the pair tracks where the process is centred; the other tracks how consistent it is.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Xbar chart plots subgroup averages, so it tracks the process **level** (centring) over time. The S chart tracks the **spread** within subgroups."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Spread is the S chart's job; capability and tolerance involve specifications, which are not on a control chart."}],final:"Xbar chart = process level → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability compares the process with specifications; a control chart does not show it.","","Incorrect — within-subgroup spread is monitored by the S chart of the pair.","Incorrect — tolerance is the width of the specification, which is never drawn on a control chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1021",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On an Xbar-S chart of fill weight, the S chart signals while the Xbar chart stays in control. What kind of change is MOST likely?",options:["A change that makes units within a subgroup less consistent, such as a worn or loose filling valve","A change in the customer's specification for fill weight","A change that affects the subgroup averages but not the spread","A change in the machine setting that moves every unit up by the same amount"],answer:0,hint:"Which chart reacts to level, and which to consistency?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **S chart** reacts to changes in **within-subgroup spread**; the **Xbar chart** reacts to changes in **level**. Level and spread usually have different causes and need different actions."},{title:"Apply",body:"An S-chart signal with a stable Xbar chart points to something making units less consistent, such as wear, looseness or mixed material."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A setting change that shifts every unit moves the level (Xbar); specifications do not affect control charts."}],final:"S-chart signal → consistency (spread) changed → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — specifications are not used on control charts, so they cannot cause a signal.","Incorrect — that describes an Xbar-chart signal, the opposite of what was observed.","Incorrect — a shift that moves every unit equally changes the level, which the Xbar chart would show."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1022",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about forming subgroups for an Xbar-S chart is CORRECT?",options:["Large subgroups remove the need to take consecutive units","Subgroups should be formed after sorting the data from smallest to largest","Each subgroup should mix all shifts so that it represents the whole day","Each subgroup should contain values produced under similar conditions over a short time"],answer:3,hint:"Rational subgrouping applies to every subgroup chart, whatever the size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **rational subgroup** holds units made under essentially the same conditions in a short time, so the S chart captures only short-term, common-cause spread."},{title:"Apply",body:"Differences between shifts, machines or batches should then show **between** subgroups, on the Xbar chart."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mixing shifts inflates s̄ and hides shifts; a large n does not cancel the rule; sorting destroys the time order."}],final:"Rational subgroups: similar conditions, short time → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — subgroup size does not change the rule; large subgroups still need units made under the same conditions.","Incorrect — sorting destroys the time order that control charts need to detect special causes.","Incorrect — mixing shifts puts between-shift differences inside the subgroup, inflating s̄ and widening the Xbar limits.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 517, 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-1023",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which of these is NOT shown on an Xbar-S chart?",options:["Control limits calculated from the average subgroup standard deviation","The specification limits of the characteristic","The grand average as the centre line of the Xbar chart","The average subgroup standard deviation as the centre line of the S chart"],answer:1,hint:"A control chart shows the voice of the process only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An Xbar-S chart shows X̿ and its limits (from s̄ and A₃) on the Xbar chart, and s̄ with its limits (B₃, B₄) on the S chart."},{title:"Find the outsider",body:"Specification limits are the voice of the customer and are never drawn on a control chart."}],final:"Specifications are not on the chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both sets of limits are calculated from s̄ with the A₃, B₃ and B₄ constants.","","Incorrect — X̿, the grand average, is the centre line of the Xbar chart.","Incorrect — s̄ is the centre line of the S chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539; Book p. 524"},{id:"sv-5-1024",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"An automated gauge records subgroups of 15 consecutive shaft diameters. The Green Belt kept an Xbar-R chart because operators are used to reading ranges. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because the range is easier for operators to understand","Yes, because Xbar-R and Xbar-S charts always give the same signals","No, because the range of 15 values uses only the two extremes, so S should be used","No, because subgroups of 15 must be charted on an I-MR chart"],answer:2,hint:"How much of a 15-value subgroup does the range actually use?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book selects **Xbar-S for subgroups of 10 or more**. The range uses only the largest and smallest of the 15 values and throws the rest away."},{title:"Apply",body:"Familiarity does not outweigh the loss of information; with software calculating S, the Xbar-S chart is the better choice."}],final:"n = 15 → Xbar-S → No → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ease of reading does not make up for ignoring 13 of the 15 values in every subgroup.","Incorrect — the two charts estimate spread differently and can give different signals, especially with large subgroups.","","Incorrect — I-MR is for one value per sample; subgroups of 15 suit an Xbar-S chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-1025",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A line moves from hand-measured subgroups of 4 to automated subgroups of 15. Put the steps for changing to an Xbar-S chart in order:

- **P** — Confirm that the new subgroup size of 15 is permanent and documented
- **Q** — Collect a new baseline of 20–25 subgroups of 15
- **R** — Calculate the grand average, s̄ and the new control limits
- **S** — Plot new subgroups against the frozen limits`,options:["P → Q → R → S","P → R → Q → S","Q → P → S → R","Q → R → P → S"],answer:0,hint:"Confirm the change, gather new data, set limits, then monitor.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Limits are recalculated only after a **documented** change (P). The new limits need a fresh baseline of subgroups at the new size (Q), from which X̿, s̄ and the limits are calculated (R)."},{title:"Then",body:"New subgroups are monitored against those frozen limits (S)."}],final:"Confirm → baseline → limits → monitor → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — limits cannot be calculated before the new baseline subgroups are collected.","Incorrect — monitoring cannot start before the new limits are calculated.","Incorrect — collects data and sets limits before confirming that the new subgroup size is permanent."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 539; Book p. 553"}];export{e as items};
